---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
// Pre-cropped 620x835, black and white, replaced 2026-09-11 (see
// DECISIONS.md) — what's exported is what ships, no second crop at render
// time, so width/height here must match the file's own actual dimensions
// exactly or Astro's image service will crop to fit whatever's declared.
// src/assets/chris-duff-source.jpg (the original colour, uncropped photo)
// is kept only in case the crop or colour treatment ever needs redoing.
import chrisDuff from '../assets/chris-duff.png';
import { CONTACT_HREF, NAV_HREFS } from '../data/nav';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About - Excel Automate | Hobart"
  description="Chris Duff has been building tools and automations in Excel for 25 years. Excel Automate brings that to businesses and non-profits across Australia."
  current="About"
>
  <section class="prose-section" style="background: var(--page);">
    <div class="about-hero" data-reveal="hero">
      <div>
        <h1 class="hero-heading">Nice to <span class="accent">meet</span> you</h1>
        <div class="hero-rule"></div>
        <p class="hero-standfirst" style="margin-bottom: 22px;">
          Excel Automate is freshly sprung in Hobart, but the person behind it has been around a while.
        </p>
        <!-- Colour was var(--text-muted) (#4A4F4C) - that grey is for
             15-16.5px text only; this paragraph is already the standard
             19px body size, which does the "quieter than the standfirst
             above" job on its own without also stepping the colour down
             (Changes - 11 September 2026.md, #6). -->
        <p style="font-size: clamp(16px, 1.8vw, 19px); line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-body); margin: 0; text-wrap: pretty;">
          Hi, I'm <span class="accent" style="font-weight: 700;">Chris</span>. I've been building tools and automations in
          Excel for 25 years across a range of different organisations, picking up plenty of database and CRM knowledge
          along the way. A degree in software engineering was the starting point, and experience has taught me a lot
          since.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-photo">
        <Image
          src={chrisDuff}
          alt="Chris Duff"
          width={620}
          height={835}
          format="webp"
          class="about-photo-img"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-section" style="background: var(--tint);">
    <div class="prose-col" data-reveal="origin">
      <h2>Where I'm coming from</h2>
      <p>
        A lot of my recent work has been with non-profit organisations, where money is better spent on doing good than
        on sophisticated systems, large IT teams, and never-ending managed services. The downside I've seen is that a
        lot of tasks get stuck being done the slow manual way, which has its own costs over time.
      </p>
      <p>
        With a business focused on finding fast and efficient custom solutions&nbsp;&thinsp;&ndash;&thinsp; something that{' '}
        <span class="accent">Modern Excel</span> is perfect for&nbsp;&thinsp;&ndash;&thinsp; I'm aiming to help more organisations across Australia
        save time, costs, and headaches.
      </p>
      <p>Being based in Hobart, I'm also keen to work with any businesses in Tasmania who might be dealing with similar issues.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-section" style="background: var(--page);">
    <div class="prose-col" data-reveal="about">
      <h2>What I'm about</h2>
      <p>
        I always enjoy solving problems, but the part of this work I like best isn't the code and the data. It's
        working with the <span class="accent">people</span> who use the systems day in day out, who labour over data and
        wrestle with spreadsheets.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: clamp(24px, 3vw, 34px);">
        Not everyone has time to become an expert at Excel, and if I can do my part to make the job easier, they can
        focus on all the other things they do to bring value.
      </p>
      <div class="pulled-quote closing">
        <p>And that's a pretty good outcome.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band cta-band--green">
    <div class="cta-band-inner" data-reveal="cta">
      <h2>Think we might be able to help with something?</h2>
      <a href={CONTACT_HREF} class="btn btn-solid-inverse">Get in touch</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band cta-band--dark">
    <div class="cta-band-inner" data-reveal="ctaLearn">
      <h2>Enough about me! Learn more about Excel and how we use it.</h2>
      <a href={NAV_HREFS['Learn more']} class="btn btn-ghost">Learn more</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-hero {
    max-width: var(--w-prose);
    margin: 0 auto;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 310px;
    gap: clamp(28px, 3.4vw, 44px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  /* .accent only gets its colour from context-scoped rules (.hero-heading
     .accent, .prose-col .accent, .cta-band--dark p .accent — the dark-panel
     one deliberately uses a brighter green for contrast, so there's no safe
     global fallback). "Chris" sits in the hero's second paragraph, outside
     both .hero-heading and .prose-col, so it had no matching rule at all and
     rendered bold-but-not-green. Same light background as those two, so the
     same colour. */
  .about-hero .accent {
    color: var(--green);
  }
  .about-photo {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 310px;
    justify-self: end;
    /* Optical alignment: the h1's glyphs start below the top of its line box
       (Manrope 800 / line-height 1.08 puts the cap height ~0.197x font-size
       down), so a box-aligned photo reads as sitting high without this nudge
       — see Design Decisions - About page.md. */
    margin-top: calc(clamp(38px, 5.2vw, 58px) * 0.2);
  }
  .about-photo-img {
    /* No aspect-ratio/object-fit/object-position - the source file is
       already the exact crop that ships (620x835), so the browser does
       nothing but scale it. Height follows the file's own ratio (~0.742)
       from the set width, landing at 310x417 in the 310px-wide container
       (Changes - 11 September 2026.md, #7). Re-add a fixed ratio only if
       this slot ever needs to hold more than one photo, e.g. a grid. */
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 20px;
    background: var(--icon-tile);
  }

  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .about-hero {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .about-photo {
      justify-self: start;
      margin-top: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
